{
  "description": "B-039 (09 §8.6, s39): what one settlement event does, as data. Each case names an order and an invitation from the fixtures (or null) and gives the event; `recorded` is false when the event row conflicted on UNIQUE (rail, event_id). `invitation` is the row the writer reads under its lock with deleted_at IS NULL, so null means none, deleted or purged (B-031, D-063). Times are UTC wall time as stored; `paidAt` defaults to the file's value. `expect.outcome` is duplicate | unknown_order | rail_mismatch | already_final | closed (with `status`) | amount_unverifiable | amount_mismatch | paid (with `activation`: activated with publishedAt and expiresAt, no_invitation, not_live, already_published, theme_mismatch). Twelve months from 29 February clamp to 28 February (lib/invitation/publish-rules.ts addMonths under the deployment's UTC). No writer sets an invitation's status to EXPIRED (expiry is read from expires_at); a row carrying it is treated as any unpublished row, as the Next.js writer always has, and that case pins it. Expected values were derived by hand from the rules and confirmed against the TypeScript; tests/payment-settlement.test.ts and services/api/tests/payment_vectors.rs run this file.",
  "paidAt": "2026-11-10T03:00:00.000Z",
